1
00:01:07,434 --> 00:01:08,731
Hello, folks at home.

2
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:12,505
I'm 100 yards from the top of Mt. Everest.

3
00:01:12,572 --> 00:01:16,133
Yet there's a part of me
that can't wait to end this silly vacation...

4
00:01:16,309 --> 00:01:19,642
...and get back to work,
where the real challenges await.

5
00:01:20,814 --> 00:01:23,339
Shouldn't you take your age
into consideration, sir?

6
00:01:23,416 --> 00:01:25,475
After all, you're 82 years old.

7
00:01:25,718 --> 00:01:27,151
Am I 82?

8
00:01:27,287 --> 00:01:31,223
These numbers mean nothing to me.
My journey through life has just begun.

9
00:01:31,357 --> 00:01:32,824
Sir! Sir!

10
00:01:33,159 --> 00:01:36,094
-Oota tells me the storm's getting worse.
-Very, very bad.

11
00:01:36,162 --> 00:01:39,427
We must abandon our ascent and
go down the mountain immediately.

12
00:01:39,499 --> 00:01:42,525
We're so very close to the top,
Mr. Puffy Jacket Man.

13
00:01:43,103 --> 00:01:46,539
I'm heading down now, sir.
I advise you to do the same.

14
00:01:46,706 --> 00:01:47,934
All right, so be it.

15
00:01:48,108 --> 00:01:51,942
See you at the staff meeting on Monday.
And I'll bring the Krispy Kremes!

16
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:13,924
There, once again, the sad footage
we've been watching all day.

17
00:02:14,567 --> 00:02:19,004
A beloved visionary who turned a single
radio station into a global media empire...

18
00:02:19,072 --> 00:02:22,564
...Preston Blake, dead at 82. More after this.

19
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:29,173
You're watching the Blake Broadcasting Network.

20
00:02:29,249 --> 00:02:31,979
The stock of Blake Media,
the parent company of this network...

21
00:02:32,051 --> 00:02:34,645
...plunged this morning
on the news of Blake's death...

22
00:02:34,721 --> 00:02:38,851
...but bounced back after a statement
by Blake's longtime number 2, Chuck Cedar.

23
00:02:38,925 --> 00:02:41,223
All the king's horses and all the king's men...

24
00:02:41,294 --> 00:02:44,730
...couldn't save Preston Blake
from becoming a human Popsicle.

25
00:02:44,797 --> 00:02:49,666
And sources indicate that Blake, who was
single and had no children, left no will.

26
00:02:50,436 --> 00:02:53,837
So who inherits Blake's 49 percent stake
in the company's stock...

27
00:02:54,007 --> 00:02:56,100
...valued at $40 billion?

28
00:02:56,442 --> 00:03:00,708
We'll discuss that with people close
to Blake, including his longtime barber...

29
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:04,714
...on tomorrow's lnside Access.
Until then, I'm Mac McGrath.

30
00:03:06,819 --> 00:03:08,684
-Are we clear?
-Clear!

31
00:03:08,988 --> 00:03:11,320
Babe? Where's Babe?

32
00:03:11,391 --> 00:03:13,882
-I'll come over and choke you to death.
-Babe!

33
00:03:14,394 --> 00:03:15,292
I got to go.

34
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:17,852
Freaking barber!
That's the best you could get me?

35
00:03:17,931 --> 00:03:20,627
People tell everything to their barbers.

36
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:24,167
He's been his closest confidant
for more than 20 years.

37
00:03:24,237 --> 00:03:28,003
I'm not putting this guy on the air.
Get me something better and get it quick.

38
00:03:28,241 --> 00:03:29,708
Or it's your ass.

39
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:35,875
Did we find this heir yet?

40
00:03:36,049 --> 00:03:38,984
We should be getting that information
via fax any minute now.

41
00:03:39,252 --> 00:03:41,447
I have lawyers looking through birth records...

42
00:03:41,521 --> 00:03:44,319
...in all 50 states, Puerto Rico and Guam.

43
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:48,884
In fact, we have a little pool going.
Guam's paying off at 1000-1.

44
00:03:49,562 --> 00:03:51,052
Is this a joke to you?

45
00:03:52,165 --> 00:03:55,657
Some genetic lottery winner
now controls the fate of this company.

46
00:03:55,735 --> 00:03:58,329
No, I agree. It's troubling.

47
00:03:58,404 --> 00:04:01,032
Troubling? Your beard is troubling.

48
00:04:01,507 --> 00:04:05,705
Your pipe is troubling.
Your yellow teeth are really troubling.

49
00:04:05,945 --> 00:04:09,608
But if I lose control of this company,
it'll be catastrophic.

50
00:04:15,488 --> 00:04:18,048
Does anyone know a Dr. Mendlesohn?

51
00:04:19,025 --> 00:04:21,323
Congratulations, you have a spastic colon.

52
00:04:22,595 --> 00:04:24,825
That would explain a lot.

53
00:04:39,812 --> 00:04:41,370
"Longfellow Deeds."

54
00:04:47,754 --> 00:04:50,382
Chuck, I think we're there.

55
00:04:52,325 --> 00:04:54,020
My God, it's all green.

56
00:04:59,766 --> 00:05:03,497
Look at that thing. Fighter pilot on your left.

57
00:05:05,204 --> 00:05:06,535
Got that sucker!

58
00:05:21,554 --> 00:05:22,782
You guys lost?

59
00:05:22,855 --> 00:05:25,653
We're looking for somebody: Longfellow Deeds.

60
00:05:25,958 --> 00:05:28,256
Is that Deeds' first name?

61
00:05:28,328 --> 00:05:30,990
If the Deeds you're referring to
is Longfellow Deeds...

62
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:33,328
...then yes, that is Deeds' first name.

63
00:05:33,533 --> 00:05:36,434
I don't know Deeds' first name. Maybe it's Greg.

64
00:05:36,769 --> 00:05:38,999
-Maybe it's Longfellow.
-Maybe.

65
00:05:39,072 --> 00:05:42,667
But I don't know. I know another guy
named Greg. You want me to call him?

66
00:05:42,742 --> 00:05:45,836
No! Thank you.
Please, just tell us where Deeds lives.

67
00:05:46,045 --> 00:05:49,947
All right, don't get all hyperactive.
I'll take you straight to him.

68
00:05:56,222 --> 00:05:57,883
Step right in, fellows.

69
00:06:01,127 --> 00:06:04,119
-He lives at a pizzeria?
-You can't live in a pizzeria.

70
00:06:04,197 --> 00:06:06,358
He lives upstairs. He owns it.

71
00:06:06,432 --> 00:06:08,730
Excuse me. Is Mr. Deeds around?

72
00:06:08,801 --> 00:06:13,261
No, I'm sorry, he's out making deliveries.
The regular delivery guy called in sick.

73
00:06:13,573 --> 00:06:18,510
-But you don't look too sick, Murph.
-I forgot I was faking sick today.

74
00:06:19,512 --> 00:06:24,142
-You two tricked me into coming here.
-Murph, put on an apron and give me a hand.

75
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,678
You guys played me like a fiddle. Touch?

76
00:06:28,721 --> 00:06:30,621
Is Mrs. Deeds around?

77
00:06:30,690 --> 00:06:33,659
Mrs. Deeds?
I don't think that poor boy ever had a date.

78
00:06:34,093 --> 00:06:37,893
-Do you know when he'll be back?
-Before lunch is out. Today's Card Day.

79
00:06:38,097 --> 00:06:40,224
-Card Day?
-Deeds writes greeting cards.

80
00:06:40,299 --> 00:06:43,462
Every Monday, he tries out a few
and then he chooses one...

81
00:06:43,536 --> 00:06:46,061
...and sends it to one of the big card companies.

82
00:06:46,139 --> 00:06:48,471
Now, he hasn't sold any yet, but...

83
00:06:48,741 --> 00:06:49,969
...he will.

84
00:06:51,110 --> 00:06:52,941
You guys need anything else here?

85
00:06:53,413 --> 00:06:54,880
"To my sweetheart

86
00:06:55,915 --> 00:06:58,577
"I love you completely, with all my soul

87
00:06:58,785 --> 00:07:01,777
"Without you I'm nothing, a butterless roll"

88
00:07:03,256 --> 00:07:04,553
That's good.

89
00:07:04,624 --> 00:07:05,886
Like that, gentlemen?

90
00:07:07,193 --> 00:07:10,060
Hallmark said it showed potential,
which is pretty cool.

91
00:07:10,129 --> 00:07:12,427
-Longfellow Deeds?
-Just call me Deeds, pal.

92
00:07:12,498 --> 00:07:14,329
Easy with that Longfellow stuff.

93
00:07:14,834 --> 00:07:16,301
Chuck Cedar, CEO.

94
00:07:16,369 --> 00:07:19,065
Cecil Anderson, General Counsel of Blake Media.

95
00:07:19,305 --> 00:07:22,138
Handshakes are for strangers.
We hug around here, buddy.

96
00:07:22,308 --> 00:07:24,435
What's up? Welcome to town.

97
00:07:25,611 --> 00:07:27,545
Come on, don't be shy.

98
00:07:28,314 --> 00:07:31,750
I like that beard. Abe Lincoln-style.
Have a seat, boys.

99
00:07:31,918 --> 00:07:33,078
-Murph!
-Deeds?

100
00:07:33,252 --> 00:07:36,187
-I thought you were sick today.
-That was a lie, pal.

101
00:07:36,289 --> 00:07:37,688
You fooled me.

102
00:07:37,757 --> 00:07:38,917
-I did?
-Yeah.

103
00:07:38,991 --> 00:07:40,925
-Mr. Deeds?
-Just Deeds.

104
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:44,955
You sounded so sick on the phone.
You're good. Do that sick voice again.

105
00:07:45,198 --> 00:07:49,157
Deeds, I can't come to work today.
I think I got strep throat.

106
00:07:52,638 --> 00:07:55,869
That's unbelievable.
You could be a radio actor or something.

107
00:07:56,108 --> 00:07:58,406
-Thanks, buddy.
-Sure.

108
00:07:58,611 --> 00:08:01,512
Hey, Deeds. Read a card already.
I got to get back on duty.

109
00:08:03,149 --> 00:08:05,674
I'd better get up there.
Good meeting you, gentlemen.

110
00:08:05,751 --> 00:08:08,652
Mr. Deeds, does the name Preston Blake
mean anything to you?

111
00:08:08,888 --> 00:08:10,879
Mom's maiden name was Blake.

112
00:08:11,224 --> 00:08:15,160
Only one this week, but I got a feeling
it'll end my losing streak with Hallmark.

113
00:08:17,663 --> 00:08:19,927
Preston Blake was your mother's uncle.

114
00:08:19,999 --> 00:08:22,991
You're kidding me, I got an uncle?
Awesome. How's he doing?

115
00:08:23,069 --> 00:08:24,297
He's dead.

116
00:08:24,770 --> 00:08:27,295
Oh, no. If there's anything I can do....

117
00:08:27,373 --> 00:08:29,102
Deeds! Deeds!

118
00:08:29,509 --> 00:08:31,136
I'll be right with you guys.

119
00:08:32,845 --> 00:08:35,279
When Mr. Blake died,
he left an enormous fortune.

120
00:08:35,548 --> 00:08:38,244
$40 billion. He left it all...

121
00:08:38,851 --> 00:08:40,284
...to you, Deeds.

122
00:08:42,588 --> 00:08:44,146
Wait a minute.

123
00:08:45,324 --> 00:08:48,953
What are you talking about, you got
to get back on duty? You're not a cop.

124
00:08:49,028 --> 00:08:51,656
You're right.
I got this down at the costume shop.

125
00:08:51,731 --> 00:08:53,494
But it can't hurt to pretend.

126
00:08:54,667 --> 00:08:56,328
What a wisenheimer!

127
00:08:56,435 --> 00:08:58,426
Okay, everybody, it's showtime.

128
00:09:00,306 --> 00:09:02,399
I call this "50th Anniversary Card."

129
00:09:03,042 --> 00:09:04,771
Which is quite a feat nowadays.

130
00:09:05,611 --> 00:09:09,342
"Fifty years have passed by
with laughter and tears

131
00:09:10,049 --> 00:09:12,279
"Do you remember when we went to the zoo

132
00:09:12,351 --> 00:09:14,717
"and that time we drank all the beers"

133
00:09:15,488 --> 00:09:16,477
Beers!

134
00:09:18,090 --> 00:09:21,526
"I promise to love you for 50 years more

135
00:09:22,028 --> 00:09:25,361
"Even when your bosoms sag down to the floor"

136
00:09:26,999 --> 00:09:29,661
That was awesome! How's he do it?

137
00:09:33,072 --> 00:09:36,007
It looks like you won't have
to deliver pizza anymore, Deeds.

138
00:09:36,475 --> 00:09:38,136
You're a very rich man.

139
00:09:38,377 --> 00:09:40,777
What's that got to do with delivering pizzas?

140
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:43,514
Ladies! The top one is yours.

141
00:09:43,583 --> 00:09:45,608
-Thank you, Deeds.
-Of course.

142
00:09:45,685 --> 00:09:47,676
-Yummy!
-So, Deeds, what's new?

143
00:09:48,321 --> 00:09:50,915
I found out I have an uncle, but he died.

144
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:52,451
That's too bad.

145
00:09:52,825 --> 00:09:56,261
-He gave me $40 billion, though.
-Well, that's nice.

146
00:09:56,462 --> 00:09:57,360
Oh, yeah!

147
00:09:57,430 --> 00:10:00,593
Don't you go and spend it all
on some fancy record player.

148
00:10:01,033 --> 00:10:02,125
I won't.

149
00:10:02,201 --> 00:10:03,793
Who are your friends, Deeds?

150
00:10:03,869 --> 00:10:08,306
I'm sorry.
Chuck, Cecil, this is Jane, Kitty and Sue.

151
00:10:08,741 --> 00:10:12,677
-Pleasure to meet you, Cecil.
-Pleasure's all mine, Kitty.

152
00:10:13,079 --> 00:10:15,673
Or should I just say, "Meow"?

153
00:10:17,817 --> 00:10:19,842
Keep it in your pants, Anderson.

154
00:10:21,153 --> 00:10:25,590
-Now, why am I inheriting this money?
-You're Mr. Blake's closest living relative.

155
00:10:25,958 --> 00:10:28,518
-Really? He had nobody else?
-Nope, that's it.

156
00:10:28,828 --> 00:10:30,728
Gosh, I never even met the guy.

157
00:10:33,733 --> 00:10:35,724
Would you hold this for a second?

158
00:10:37,036 --> 00:10:39,231
Mr. Wetherley. How you doing, pal?

159
00:10:39,438 --> 00:10:42,498
Very well, Deeds. Just going to the pharmacy.

160
00:10:42,575 --> 00:10:44,907
Well, let me get you there a little quicker.

161
00:10:48,114 --> 00:10:50,275
-How's your wife?
-She's fine.

162
00:10:50,349 --> 00:10:52,817
-Terrific. Say hi for me.
-I will.

163
00:10:52,885 --> 00:10:54,250
Okay. Here you go.

164
00:10:55,588 --> 00:10:58,318
-Thanks for the lift, Deeds.
-You got it.

165
00:11:01,193 --> 00:11:05,095
We need you to come back to New York with us.
Just for a few days.

166
00:11:05,197 --> 00:11:07,631
We're drawing up some papers
you'll need to sign.

167
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:08,928
What kind of papers?

168
00:11:09,301 --> 00:11:13,795
Technically, you've inherited
300 million shares of Blake Media, lnc.

169
00:11:13,873 --> 00:11:17,309
Chuck and I, along with
some of the partners, will buy that stock...

170
00:11:17,376 --> 00:11:20,641
...for $40 billion,
which we've had to borrow from various--

171
00:11:20,713 --> 00:11:22,874
Who cares about the financial mumbo jumbo?

172
00:11:22,948 --> 00:11:25,746
We need a little time
to dot the t's and cross the i's.

173
00:11:25,951 --> 00:11:29,250
And while we're doing that,
we need you to stay away from the press.

174
00:11:29,321 --> 00:11:32,552
The media frenzy will die down
in a couple weeks but until then....

175
00:11:32,625 --> 00:11:36,425
You don't want reporters crawling
all over this lovely little town, do you?

176
00:11:36,862 --> 00:11:37,851
Oh, no!

177
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:43,164
-Crazy Eyes!
-What?

178
00:11:46,172 --> 00:11:49,266
How you doing, pal?
I got your pizza, just the way you like it.

179
00:11:49,775 --> 00:11:50,764
Oh, yes!

180
00:11:51,944 --> 00:11:55,004
French fries and Oreos.
You know me all too well, Deeds.

181
00:11:55,381 --> 00:11:56,643
What are you in for?

182
00:11:56,716 --> 00:11:59,184
I'm doing a one-nighter
for biting Ed the mailman.

183
00:11:59,485 --> 00:12:02,921
The guy was trying to cast a spell on me,
like a wizard.

184
00:12:04,056 --> 00:12:07,890
-Are you sure about that?
-I don't know. Maybe he was just waving.

185
00:12:09,428 --> 00:12:12,989
-Who are your friends?
-This is Chuck and Cecil from New York.

186
00:12:14,467 --> 00:12:15,695
I don't like them.

187
00:12:16,702 --> 00:12:18,033
Okay. Nice seeing you.

188
00:12:19,271 --> 00:12:23,002
I've never been to New York before.
I suppose I could check out the sights.

189
00:12:23,109 --> 00:12:25,441
-Give me an hour to pack, okay?
-You got it.

190
00:12:25,511 --> 00:12:29,470
I'll be the first guy to leave here since
Jimmy McNally went to Disney World.

191
00:12:31,984 --> 00:12:33,474
See you later, everybody!

192
00:12:36,956 --> 00:12:40,517
Tomorrow is "Kids Eat For Free" Day.
Don't forget to clean the highchairs.

193
00:12:40,593 --> 00:12:42,993
Don't worry, I'll take care of everything.

194
00:12:43,062 --> 00:12:45,257
-Go, have a good time!
-I'll miss you.

195
00:12:45,331 --> 00:12:47,424
'Bye, Jan. Smell you later, Murph.

196
00:12:49,268 --> 00:12:50,667
-Give us a card!
-Yeah!

197
00:12:53,572 --> 00:12:57,338
"On my way to the big city for a business trip

198
00:12:57,843 --> 00:13:02,041
"Never, ever left Mandrake Falls before
Ain't that a pip

199
00:13:04,817 --> 00:13:08,548
"But while I'm gone, I know I'll miss you all a lot"

200
00:13:09,021 --> 00:13:12,923
"So bring your rich butt back here
and buy us all a shot"

201
00:13:14,794 --> 00:13:16,284
You got it, pal!

202
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:22,199
Take it easy.

203
00:13:30,810 --> 00:13:34,769
The biggest story of the year
and we've got nothing?

204
00:13:35,381 --> 00:13:38,908
Do you people want to be tabloid journalists?
Because I don't think so.

205
00:13:39,285 --> 00:13:42,152
You've got to be ruthless. When I started out...

206
00:13:42,221 --> 00:13:45,122
...I slept in Tom and Roseanne's dumpster
for two days...

207
00:13:45,191 --> 00:13:48,024
-...disguised as a giant carton of ice cream.
-I got it!

208
00:13:48,727 --> 00:13:51,218
My source at Blake says
the company helicopter...

209
00:13:51,297 --> 00:13:54,755
...took off from there this afternoon
with two male passengers aboard...

210
00:13:54,834 --> 00:13:56,802
-...headed for Mandrake Falls.
-Where?

211
00:13:56,869 --> 00:13:59,167
Some little hick-ass town in New Hampshire.

212
00:13:59,238 --> 00:14:03,004
But now they're on their way back
with three male passengers.

213
00:14:03,709 --> 00:14:05,700
-That's one more!
-Duh!

214
00:14:06,712 --> 00:14:09,681
That third guy's our heir. Nice work, Marty.

215
00:14:09,748 --> 00:14:12,182
They were supposed to arrive
at Blake Media at 5:00.

216
00:14:12,251 --> 00:14:14,481
But they made an unscheduled stop
in Connecticut.

217
00:14:15,054 --> 00:14:17,284
I'm so happy I got the Big Bacon Classic.

218
00:14:17,590 --> 00:14:19,820
Thanks for stopping. This is unbelievable.

219
00:14:19,892 --> 00:14:23,020
-Happy to do it.
-How's that Frosty treating you, Cecil?

220
00:14:23,596 --> 00:14:26,861
You got some on your beard. I'm just kidding.

221
00:14:26,932 --> 00:14:28,661
I got him big time, man.

222
00:14:29,602 --> 00:14:31,832
I'm going on the air in two hours' time.

223
00:14:32,171 --> 00:14:35,937
And I want that guy's life story by then.
I'm talking to you, Jim.

224
00:14:36,408 --> 00:14:38,808
And I'm also talking to you, Babe. Babe?

225
00:14:39,879 --> 00:14:41,210
Where the hell's Babe?

226
00:14:41,580 --> 00:14:44,344
You missed another staff meeting. Mac's pissed.

227
00:14:44,416 --> 00:14:47,817
He won't be pissed when I crack this Blake story.

228
00:14:47,887 --> 00:14:51,084
-What do you got?
-Nothing. Do you have anything?

229
00:14:51,423 --> 00:14:53,414
I'll have a lot more in a few hours.

230
00:14:53,492 --> 00:14:54,925
You got to be a pal.

231
00:14:54,994 --> 00:14:58,589
I need this story.
I'm flat broke and Mac is going to fire me.

232
00:14:58,664 --> 00:15:01,599
How're you flat broke?
You make more money than anyone here.

233
00:15:01,667 --> 00:15:03,532
-What did you blow it on?
-Shoes.

234
00:15:04,036 --> 00:15:06,527
I'll tell you everything I know
